<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Wu of Chen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">503–559</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Chen Baxian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Wu of Chen, born Chen Baxian, was a Liang general who suppressed the rising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-nam-de">Lý Bí</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in 545 and helped defeat the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hou-jing">Hou Jing</a>. Taking control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>, he deposed the last Liang emperor in 557 and founded the Chen dynasty, the last of the southern dynasties before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s reunification under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-chen.jpg" alt="Emperor Wu of Chen" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Wu_of_Chen2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chen Baxian was born in 503 to Chen Wenzan and Lady Dong in Wuxing Commandery, roughly modern Huzhou in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, in a family that traced its descent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> scholar Chen Shi and had long served as officials. As a young man he was thought ambitious and studied military strategy and fighting techniques. In the late 530s Xiao Ying, a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liang">Emperor Wu of Liang</a> and governor of Wuxing, was impressed by him and around 540 took him onto his staff in Guang Province, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Lý Bôn's rebellion in Giao Châu crushed a Liang army in 542 and the defeated generals' kin attacked the provincial capital to avenge them, Chen defeated the attackers and was made Viscount of Xin'an. In 545, as lieutenant to the governor Yang Piao, he defeated Lý Bôn, and in 548 he defeated Lý Bôn's brother Lý Thiên Bảo.</p><p>After the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hou-jing">Hou Jing</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> in 549, Chen marched north through modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a>, pledging loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yuan-of-liang">Xiao Yi</a>, the Prince of Xiangdong. In 552 he and Xiao Yi's general Wang Sengbian besieged Jiankang and drove Hou out. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangling">Jiangling</a> and killed Xiao Yi, by then Emperor Yuan, in 554, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a> forced Xiao Yuanming on Liang as emperor. In autumn 555 Chen, thinking him unworthy, made a surprise attack on Wang Sengbian at Shitou, killed him and his son, and installed Emperor Yuan's son Xiao Fangzhi as Emperor Jing. He then fought off Wang's kin, rival generals and two Northern Qi attacks on the capital, defeating the second in summer 556.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Raised step by step to Prince of Chen, he had Emperor Jing yield the throne to him in winter 557. Once emperor he gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> official sanction, displaying a relic believed to be a tooth of the Buddha and, following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liang">Emperor Wu of Liang</a>, once offering himself to the Buddha's service. The general Wang Lin refused to accept him, captured his generals Zhou Wenyu and Hou Andu, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a>'s help declared Xiao Zhuang emperor. In summer 558 Emperor Wu had the former Emperor Jing killed, and he later negotiated a peace with Wang Lin.</p><p>His son Chen Chang and nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xuan-of-chen">Chen Xu</a> had been taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> as captives in 554, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> never returned them in his lifetime despite his requests. When he died suddenly in 559, Empress Zhang kept his death secret until his nephew Chen Qian could return to the capital, and the officials made Chen Qian emperor as Emperor Wen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Wu of Chen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The general <b>Chen Baxian</b>, who had defeated Hou Jing, gained control of Jiankang and in 557 accepted the abdication of the boy Emperor Jing of Liang, founding Chen with himself as Emperor Wu.</blockquote><a href="/state/chen-dynasty">Chen dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Liang forces under <b>Chen Baxian</b> defeated him in 545, but resistance continued under Trieu Quang Phuc and later Ly Phat Tu, who held the delta for decades through a mix of guerrilla warfare and negotiation.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-ly-dynasty">Early Ly dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>In 557 <b>Chen Baxian</b> deposed the last emperor and founded the Chen dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/liang-dynasty">Liang dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
